Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Fix HTML/CSS injection in the Pin-Down confirmation page
 generator
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Scrutinized the confirmation-page pipeline (Pin-Down's show-rate setup:
the branded post-booking page a client's prospects land on, published
to their real Webflow/WordPress/Vercel site — see page-builder.ts's old
docstring). Found two real injection gaps in content-model.ts and
tokens.ts, the shared code all 5 templates (static + dynamic) build on.

1. fontFamily (live, reachable via the normal onboarding flow whenever
   a design scrape succeeds): tokens.ts's own header comment states the
   design goal as "never trust scraped signal with real output — pick
   from a small pre-vetted catalog instead." Every classifier in that
   file honors it except this one, which carries fontFamilyMentions[0]
   verbatim into a template's <style> block. extractFontFamilyMentions's
   regex (design-scraper.ts) stops at `;`/`"`/`'`/`}` but not `<`/`>`, so
   a "font-family:" match anywhere in a scraped page's raw HTML could
   carry `</style><script>...` straight into the published page. Fixed
   with an allowlist (letters/digits/spaces/hyphens only) in tokens.ts —
   the actual boundary before HTML embedding — plus tightening the
   source regex in design-scraper.ts as defense in depth.

2. calendarAddToUrl: every other field in buildPageContentModel() is
   HTML-escaped once, centrally, before any template touches it — this
   one field, the only one that's a real href rather than text content,
   was passed through raw. All 10 templates then interpolate it directly
   into an href attribute with no escaping of their own. Not reachable
   today (onboarding-service.ts's one call site never populates it), but
   the field is live in the type surface for whenever something starts
   wiring a real "add to calendar" link — silently reactivating an XSS
   on a client's own published site. Escaping alone doesn't stop a
   javascript:/data: URI either, so this adds a scheme allowlist
   (http/https/mailto) ahead of the existing escapeHtml() call. Also
   added the missing single-quote case to escapeHtml() itself, matching
   every other attribute-safety escaper.

Deleted page-builder.ts — a dead, unused duplicate of this same
build-the-confirmation-page logic (superseded by templates/ +
content-model.ts, confirmed via grep: nothing imports it, only two
comments reference it by name). Ironically the one copy that escaped
calendarAddToUrl correctly; left in place it was a landmine for anyone
who assumed it was the live implementation during a future review.

